  "summary": "Shah-kul birds, linked hands and horn motifs in madder-red flatweave — Serbia's kilim capital.",
  "description": "Pirot weavers — mostly women, weaving in family workshops on big low-warp looms — turned the kilim into Serbian national treasure: strong madder reds, brick and gold grounds covered in named motifs like the šah-kuliča (birds of paradise), kuke (hooks), begovi (linked wealthy hands) and turtles, each woven with two wefts so the pattern shows identically on both faces. Pirot kilims carpeted Ottoman houses, Balkan churches and, in the 20th century, Belgrade royal palaces and ocean-liner salons. The geometric vocabulary is so codified that weavers speak of motifs by name like a language of the loom.",
  "culturalContext": "Pirot kilims carpeted Ottoman houses, Serbian royal courts and ocean liners; weaving knowledge (motif names spoken like a language) passes through women's family workshops. The carpet is Pirot's geographic-indication product and a Serbian national symbol — woven into Belgrade's state gifts and diaspora weddings.",
